{
  "decision": "no_change",
  "headline": "Current plans support 2027 custom growth, not the expert’s one-third share; NVIDIA base held at $371.43bn",
  "note": "The former planner expects accelerator spending to outgrow total capex and custom silicon to take roughly one-third of new accelerator dollars at two clouds by late 2027. Knowledge ends in 2025 at one employer, and the expert supplies no 2026 figure, employer identity or current budget crosswalk [email-expert].\n\nCurrent disclosures support identifiable custom-silicon expansion: Amazon’s April release names approximately 2GW of OpenAI Trainium capacity beginning to ramp in 2027, reaffirmed directionally in July [amzn-2026-04-29-release] [amzn-2026-07-30-release]. Alphabet’s Q2 filing describes external TPU supply agreements with most revenue expected in 2027 [web-fb40041ca0]. These are independent company disclosures, not confirmation of the expert’s unnamed plans or one-third estimate.\n\nNeither gives comparable 2027 accelerator-spending and total-capex budgets. GW and chip counts are not dollars; Amazon’s chips revenue includes CPUs and Nitro, while external TPU sales are not Alphabet’s own capex. Meta’s future leases and Alphabet’s multi-year construction also limit a blanket buildings-pulled-forward inference [web-b8b374a1b1] [web-fb40041ca0].\n\nI retain every 2026 input and NVIDIA Q3/Q4 estimates of $102bn/$127bn. The prior custom-silicon tail adjustment and dated-slip allowance already cover stronger, more current evidence; this historical expectation warrants no additional haircut. Named procurement dollars and recognition schedules would move me.\n\nNVIDIA’s long calendar earnings case is unchanged this session: base remains $371.43bn, $4.57bn below street; AMD remains approximately at $14bn consensus.",
  "stance": "Capex budgets exceed street, but deployment slips weaken NVIDIA’s calendar capture; 2027 custom growth lacks a measured spending share.",
  "rationale": "Partly agree with direction, not quantified share or relative growth. Current named Trainium/TPU disclosures provide a stronger basis than dated single-employer recollection, but no 2026 bridge or comparable 2027 spending denominator. No further probability shift beyond existing custom and delivery allowances is defensible from this item. Move on current customer budgets, custom/total accelerator dollars or supplier recognition schedules.",
  "thesis": "Hold calendar-2026 capex $682.5bn versus $667.5bn street; NVIDIA DC base $371.43bn versus $376bn (-$4.57bn, -1.22%), AMD GPU approximately $14bn versus $14bn. Base mix/split/concentration remain 37.3%/65%/44.55% for NVIDIA, AMD split/concentration 2.75%/50%. NVIDIA unreported Q3 DC $102bn and own Q4 $127bn versus required $126.58bn remain on record. Directional 2027 custom growth is supported, but no quantified 2027 share forecast is adopted.",
